usage : tty-clock [-iuvsScbtrahDBxn] [-C [0-7]] [-f format] [-d delay] [-a nsdelay] [-T tty] [--theme default|nord] [--copilot] [--copilot-refresh seconds]
    --theme name  Set the color theme (default or nord)
    --copilot     Show Copilot usage below the date (P toggles)
    --copilot-refresh seconds  Refresh interval, default 60 (1..86400)
    -s            Show seconds
    -S            Screensaver mode
    -x            Show box
    -c            Set the clock at the center of the terminal
    -C [0-7]      Set the clock color
    -b            Use bold colors
    -t            Set the hour in 12h format
    -u            Use UTC time
    -T tty        Display the clock on the specified terminal
    -r            Do rebound the clock
    -f format     Set the date format
    -n            Don't quit on keypress
    -v            Show tty-clock version
    -i            Show some info about tty-clock
    -h            Show this page
    -D            Hide date
    -B            Enable blinking colon
    -d delay      Set the delay between two redraws of the clock. Default 1s.
    -a nsdelay    Additional delay between two redraws in nanoseconds. Default 0ns.

Nord theme
----------
Run a centered Nord clock with seconds:

    ./tty-clock -c -s --theme nord
    ./tty-clock -c -s --theme nord --copilot

Nord uses a Polar Night background (#2E3440), Frost clock and filled usage
bar (#88C0D0), Snow Storm date and usage text (#D8DEE9), and muted borders.
The background fills the terminal while the clock runs. Terminals supporting
palette changes use exact Nord RGB colors; other terminals use the nearest
available colors. --theme default selects the normal terminal colors.

With Nord active, -C and keys 0-7 select these clock/bar accents:
0 muted, 1 red, 2 green, 3 yellow, 4 blue, 5 purple, 6 frost (default), 7 white.
An explicit -C works before or after --theme. Date and usage text stay light.

Verification (after building): python3 tests/test_theme.py

Copilot usage
-------------
Build with make (requires a C compiler and ncurses development headers).
On Ubuntu/Debian: sudo apt install build-essential libncurses-dev pkg-config

Install GitHub CLI (gh), authenticate with gh auth login on github.com, then run:

    ./tty-clock -c -s --copilot

Below the date, the clock displays a bar filled by the percentage consumed,
with the actual usage on a separate left-aligned line, in the clock's color:

      Copilot [######--------------] 30.5% used
      60,847.1 / 200,000 credits

      Today UTC: ~8,200 / 6,100 (34% over)
      Today [####################] 102.5% of daily avg

      Safe avg: 6,000 credits/day
      Recent avg: 8,000/day (33.3% over safe pace)
      Est. limit by 01 Dec: 220,847.1 credits (+10.4%)
      Empty: 24 Nov 14:30 UTC; ~7 days early

The block is centered beneath the clock with a shared left edge. Blank rows
separate monthly quota, today's usage and the forecast.

The bar uses solid background-colored cells when running. Amounts are shown to one
decimal place when needed, with thousands separators. Credit-based accounts
show credits; legacy premium-request accounts show requests. Where available,
actual usage is calculated from entitlement minus quota_remaining, since the
API's integer credits_used can be rounded. Unlimited allowances show usage
without a percentage or denominator when the server supplies it.

The daily budget divides the allowance remaining at the start of the UTC day
by the calendar days left, including today. It stays fixed during that day.
The next reset is assumed to be the first of the next month at 00:00 UTC;
budget days also use UTC, independently of the clock's -u option.
The Today line shows headroom or the percentage over today's fixed budget.
The second graph compares measured today's consumption with the estimated
daily average: 100% means one average day's usage, not 100% of the safe budget.
The bar fills at 100%, but the percentage can exceed 100%. Incomplete days
use "Since HH:MM" and graph only consumption observed since that UTC time.
If the average is missing or zero, the graph says it is not available.
The graph shrinks on narrow terminals and uses the same accent as the monthly bar.
"Safe avg" is the maximum average daily consumption the remaining allowance
can support until reset, based on the latest measurement. "Month avg" or
"Recent avg" shows your actual average and explicitly says whether that pace
is within budget or how far above the safe pace it is. The safe average
changes as you consume quota; it is distinct from today's fixed budget.
Forecasts use observed consumption over up to seven days (at least 24 hours),
including time offline. Until enough history exists, "Month avg" uses usage
divided by elapsed time this month (after the first hour). "At this pace" means
the remaining allowance should last until reset at that average rate;
"Empty" estimates when it will run out and how many days (or hours) early.
The early interval is rounded up and marked approximate. These are estimates,
not guarantees, and GitHub's accounting can lag behind actual activity.
"Est. limit by" shows the suggested total quota limit: usage already consumed
plus estimated usage until the displayed reset date at the same daily average,
but never less than the current limit. Only the shortfall beyond the remaining
allowance is added to the current limit. The percentage in parentheses is the
required increase relative to the current limit, not the daily pace overage;
"no increase" means the current limit is sufficient. Both the future-usage
and run-out estimates stay anchored to the latest successful reading if stale.

Only monthly totals are available from the API. Local history is stored in
$XDG_STATE_HOME/tty-clock/copilot-history (default ~/.local/state/tty-clock),
with private file permissions, a nonblocking lock and atomic replacement.
No credentials are stored. First-day or interrupted daily tracking shows
"Today: unknown" and the amount observed since a displayed UTC start time:
usage before the first reading is unknown. The average/safe pace comparison
works after the first hour of the month using the month's totals. Readings spanning midnight
within five minutes give approximate full-day tracking, marked "~".
Resets, allowance/unit changes and downward corrections restart tracking.
History is for one GitHub account; remove this history file when switching
accounts with the same allowance. Unwritable storage falls back to session
tracking, marked "[no save]". Unlimited or unavailable quotas have no forecast.

Press P to toggle the display. --copilot-refresh 120 changes the refresh interval
to two minutes. Requests run in the background with a 15-second timeout,
without client-side caching, and request revalidation of cached responses.
Intervals are measured between request starts, rather than after completion.
GitHub may still take time to account for recent usage. Requests do
not interrupt the clock or keyboard input. Failed refreshes keep the previous
reading marked stale; unavailable data shows an authentication hint. A missing
premium allowance is treated as unavailable, not as zero usage. With -D the
usage appears directly below the clock. On small terminals the bar shrinks and
long text is clipped safely. Existing screensaver key-exit behavior still applies.

This uses gh api --hostname github.com /copilot_internal/user with the existing
gh credentials. No browser cookies are needed. The endpoint is internal to
GitHub and may change. Only enabling the display starts quota requests.

Verification (after building): python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
The tests use a stub gh and pseudo-terminals; they do not access GitHub.
